Maybe playing the game isn’t about knowing what’s going to happen. It’s about participating in what happens next.

You don’t have to understand every rule to play. And sometimes you only discover the rules by breaking your expectations.

There’s a beautiful paradox there:

You cannot fully know a game you’re helping create.

Every move changes the board. Every player changes the possibilities. Even choosing not to play can influence the outcome.

And perhaps mastery isn’t controlling every outcome.

It’s understanding when to follow the rules, when to change your strategy, when to create something new, and when to realize you’ve been playing a different game altogether.

The game isn’t separate from reality. Playing is part of how reality unfolds.

And here’s my question for you:

What if the unexpected isn’t an interruption of the game?

What if the unexpected is the game?

